<title>USA: Germany should stick with mark pre-EMU - DB Bank.</title>
<headline>Germany should stick with mark pre-EMU - DB Bank.</headline>
<dateline>WASHINGTON 1996-09-29</dateline>
<text>
<p>Germany's DG Bank Deutsche Genossenschaftsbank said Sunday it advised investors against speculating on a delay in the launch of a single European currency and said they should retain investments in marks.</p>
<p>&quot;Our advice to customers is not to speculate on a delay in monetary union,&quot; Bernd Thiemann, the bank's chief executive, told a news conference on the sidelines of the annual meeting of the International Monetary Fund and World Bank.</p>
<p>Management board member Uwe Flach told the news conference interest rates among &quot;hard core&quot; EMU nations were already stable.</p>
<p>&quot;The interest rate premium for hard core countries compared with the mark is next to nothing,&quot; he said. &quot;That means one should stay in marks.&quot;</p>
<p>--Janet Northcote, Washington Newsroom +1 202 898 8300</p>
